Template: mctop/install-sudoers
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Set up a sudoers rule so mctop can read the Java heap?
 mctop reads heap occupancy with the JDK's jstat and jcmd. Those tools can only
 attach to a JVM as the user that owns it: jstat reads a file the JVM creates
 mode 0600, and jcmd attaches over a socket that checks the caller's user and
 group. Belonging to the server user's group is not enough.
 .
 If mctop will run as a different account from the Minecraft server, a rule in
 /etc/sudoers.d/mctop can let it invoke just those two tools as the server's
 user, without granting anything else.
 .
 Declining is safe. Everything except the Java heap and garbage collector
 panels still works, and you can add the rule later with
 "sudo dpkg-reconfigure mctop".

Template: mctop/server-user
Type: string
Default: minecraft
Description: User account that runs the Minecraft server:
 The rule will allow the JDK tools to be run as this user. It is the account
 named by User= in the server's systemd unit, and the owner of the server's
 java process.

Template: mctop/admin-user
Type: string
Description: Account that will run mctop:
 The rule will be granted to this account. Give a user name, or a group name
 prefixed with a percent sign, such as %minecraft-admins.
 .
 Leaving this empty skips the rule.

Template: mctop/tools-not-found
Type: error
Description: JDK tools not found
 jstat and jcmd could not be found, and a sudoers rule has to name them by
 absolute path. No rule has been written.
 .
 Install a JDK — default-jdk-headless will do — and then run
 "sudo dpkg-reconfigure mctop" to try again.

Template: mctop/sudoers-invalid
Type: error
Description: The sudoers rule was rejected
 visudo refused the generated rule, so it has not been installed. This is a
 bug in mctop's packaging; please report it. Nothing has been changed, and
 sudo is unaffected.
